## 5. Domain Model

### 5.1 Validation limits and enums

Use Zod schemas and TypeScript types from a single domain definition.

Limits:

- Maximum 30 exercises per workout or routine.
- `exerciseId` must be unique within a workout.
- `exerciseId` must be unique within a routine.
- Maximum 20 sets per exercise.
- Name maximum 100 characters.
- Notes maximum 1,000 characters.
- Reps: integer from 0 to 1,000.
- Weight: 0 to 1,000 kg.
- RPE: 1 to 10, in increments of 0.5.
- `completedLocalDate` must be a valid `YYYY-MM-DD` date and within
  approximately one day of `completedAt` as a sanity check.

An exercise may only be added once to a workout in the MVP. Supporting
duplicate exercise entries in one workout can be considered later.

Muscle group enum:

```text
chest, back, shoulders, biceps, triceps, forearms, quads,
hamstrings, glutes, calves, core, full_body, cardio, other
```

Equipment enum:

```text
barbell, dumbbell, machine, cable, bodyweight, kettlebell, band, other
```

### 5.2 Exercises

Exercise attributes:

```ts
interface Exercise {
  exerciseId: string;
  name: string;
  muscleGroup: MuscleGroup;
  equipment: Equipment;
  isCustom: boolean;
  createdAt: string;
  updatedAt: string;
  deletedAt?: string;
}
```

Requirements:

- Include approximately 50--100 common built-in exercises.
- Built-in exercise IDs use stable slugs, for example
  `barbell-bench-press`.
- Built-in exercises are read-only in the MVP.
- Users can create, edit, and soft-delete their own custom exercises.
- Soft-deleted custom exercises are excluded from normal pickers but
  remain resolvable by ID.
- Existing workout history remains readable because workouts snapshot
  exercise names.
- Maximum 200 active custom exercises. Soft-deleted exercises do not
  count toward the cap.

### 5.3 Routines

```ts
interface Routine {
  routineId: string;
  name: string;
  exercises: RoutineExercise[];
  createdAt: string;
  updatedAt: string;
}

interface RoutineExercise {
  exerciseId: string;
  order: number;
  targetSets: number;
  targetReps: number;
}
```

Requirements:

- Full create, read, update and delete functionality.
- Preserve exercise ordering.
- Exercise IDs must be unique within a routine.
- Maximum 30 exercises per routine.
- Maximum 50 routines.
- Routine exercises refer to exercise IDs. Handle soft-deleted or
  unavailable exercises gracefully.

### 5.4 Workouts

Workout IDs are client-generated ULIDs. They provide stable identifiers
and roughly time-sortable ordering.

```ts
interface Workout {
  workoutId: string;
  routineId?: string;
  name: string;
  startedAt: string;
  completedAt?: string;
  completedLocalDate?: string;
  status: "active" | "completed";
  exercises: WorkoutExercise[];
  createdAt: string;
  updatedAt: string;
}

interface WorkoutExercise {
  exerciseId: string;
  name: string;
  order: number;
  sets: WorkoutSet[];
}

interface WorkoutSet {
  setId: string;
  type: "normal" | "warmup" | "drop" | "failure";
  reps?: number;
  weightKg?: number;
  completed: boolean;
  rpe?: number;
  notes?: string;
}
```

Rules:

- Store weight canonically in kilograms.
- Convert to/from pounds only at the UI boundary according to the
  user's preference.
- Never store both kg and lb values.
- Snapshot exercise names into workouts so history remains readable if
  an exercise is renamed.
- Exclude warm-up sets from PR calculations.
- Completed workouts are immutable in the MVP.
- An active workout is stored in the same JSON document as all other
  app data.
- Workout IDs remain stable throughout the active workout lifecycle.

### 5.5 Exercise history

Do not store a denormalised exercise-history collection in the JSON
document.

The workout list is the source of truth. Exercise-specific history is
derived by selecting completed workouts containing the relevant
exercise.

This avoids duplicate data and removes the need to keep separate history
records in sync when workouts are deleted or changed.

Sort history consistently by workout start time. Display completion time
and local completion date where relevant.

### 5.6 Statistics

Calculate locally:

```ts
interface UserStats {
  totalWorkouts: number;
  lastWorkoutLocalDate?: string;
  currentStreakDays: number;
}
```

Statistics are derived from completed workouts rather than separately
persisted as authoritative data.

Streak = consecutive calendar days (using the local date recorded on
completion) with at least one completed workout.

Streak rules when completing a workout on local date `D`, with previous
last date `L`:

- `D = L` or `D < L`: streak and last workout date remain unchanged;
  total still increments.
- `D = L + 1`: increment streak and set last workout date to `D`.
- `D > L + 1`: reset streak to 1 and set last workout date to `D`.

When deleting a completed workout, recalculate the statistics from the
remaining completed workouts.

The displayed streak is zero if the last workout date is older than
yesterday. Apply this stale-streak rule at read time.

For a single-user JSON document, recalculating from the complete local
history is acceptable initially. Avoid introducing the original
server-side 200-workout recomputation limit.

### 5.7 Personal records

Calculate PRs per exercise from completed, non-warm-up sets:

1.  **Heaviest weight:** greatest weight lifted for at least one rep.
2.  **Best estimated 1RM:** Epley formula, `weight * (1 + reps / 30)`,
    for sets of 1--12 reps.
3.  **Most reps:** greatest reps in a single set when no weight is
    recorded (bodyweight).

PRs are calculated client-side from local workout history. They do not
need separate persisted records for the MVP.

